 on  um it goes on to say the new guidelines  wearing a mask is most important if you  have a weakened immune system or if  because of your age or an underlying  medical condition  you are at increased risk for severe  disease or if someone in your household  has a weakened immune system  is at increased risk for severe disease  or is unvaccinated if this applies to  you  or your household you might choose to  wear a mask regardless of the level of  transmission in your area  you should continue to wear a mask where  required by laws  rules regulations or local guidance  so this is a significant  change and we are seeing the spike i  mean if you look at the u.s  case numbers you will see that starting  oh  i don't know right around june 22nd  about about a month ago after getting  down below 10  000 cases per day cases started to go up  up  up including even some 65 000 uh  case days now up to an average of about  57  000 cases per day now what's the hope  here  the hope is that much like we are  starting to see  in some other countries for example if  you look at the uk  which was essentially one of the hubs of  the delta variant  we have already apparently hit the peak  of cases in the uk  that the peak in the uk of delta  seems to have happened around july 22nd  and they are now down from about 46 000  cases a day  to about 32 000 that's a significant  reduction the hope is  that what is now a sort of leveling out  as you can  see over the last few days the cases  aren't continuing to increase in the us  they're sort of  flattening out the hope would be we are  now going to be  on the decline but we don't know if we  are and part of that depends of course  on taking precautions so let's not go  crazy one way or the other  i think there's a lot of hyperbole on  both sides here and i'm not  playing a both sides game the people  responsible for this  are overwhelmingly the unvaccinated  people and the people taking no  precautions  but the reactions have been sort o